    {
      "obligation": "refuter:14:1",
      "id": "weak-elliptic-maximum-principles-and-holder-regularity",
      "location": "Summary prose, final sentence",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The page says the local boundedness, oscillation reduction, Hölder regularity, Moser, weak Harnack, and Harnack results have constants depending only on “n,q,θ,M_a and the stated radii.” But the scoped local-boundedness theorem states C=C(n,θ,M_a,ρ,p). The p-dependence cannot be omitted: on B₁⊂ℝ², u(x)=x₁⁺ is a nonnegative weak subsolution of −Δu=0, with ess sup on B₁/₂ equal to 1/2, while (avg_{B₁}u^p)^{1/p}→0 as p↓0. Thus no constant independent of p gives the stated estimate.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "09a0cbff0880716ca767ffaa7440db0b7c0b5df957f9f5f45e0ab06f193ecdf8"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:14:2",
      "id": "lem-de-giorgi-oscillation-reduction",
      "location": "Step 1.1",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The first claim covers every B_R(x₀)⊂⊂Ω, but Step 1.1 defines v(y)=(u(x₀+Ry)−(M+m)/2)/L for y∈B₂(0), which requires B_{2R}(x₀)⊂Ω, a condition not assumed for that claim. The dichotomy itself follows directly from the disjoint half-level sets on B_R, so this is an immediately closable proof gap.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "4e14c9c1f5dce0263176e7cbe78956d55a7a4d2ba3ae4c343f92c47b9e1b2b02"
